# Every fixture, golden and input alike. The goldens are compared byte for byte and the renderers
# emit `\n` on every platform; without this a checkout on Windows — where git ships with
# `core.autocrlf=true`, as GitHub's runners do — rewrites them to CRLF and every line differs. The
# inputs are pinned for the matching reason: what a conversion produces must not depend on the
# platform the repository was checked out on.
#
# Stated over the whole directory rather than per extension, because naming extensions is what
# failed. `*.report.md` and `tests/fixtures/*.csv` covered every fixture when they were written;
# then `.report.txt` and `.golden.txt` goldens were added and never listed, and the CSVs moved down
# a level into `sessions/`, where a single `*` no longer reaches them — it does not cross a `/`.
# Neither omission had to touch this file in order to compile, and neither shows on Linux, so the
# gap surfaced only on the Windows leg of a release build.
tests/fixtures/** text eol=lf

# Except the workbook, which is a zip: line-ending conversion would corrupt it. Git's own heuristic
# already treats it as binary, but the rule above is explicit, so this has to be too.
tests/fixtures/**/*.xlsx binary

# And except the Green Button feeds, which are stored with the CRLF Toronto Hydro serves them with.
# `-text` is "convert nothing, in either direction", which is not the same as `eol=lf`: these are
# specimens cut from a real export, and normalising them would make the fixtures differ from the
# bytes the reader actually meets in the field. It fixes the Windows checkout just as well — the
# reason CRLF is a problem elsewhere is that it appears on one platform and not the other, and a
# file git never converts is identical everywhere.
tests/fixtures/**/*.XML -text

# The app icon: embedded into the Windows executables by build.rs and into the GUI binary by
# `include_bytes!`. Marked binary so that no checkout can rewrite a byte of it.
assets/* binary
